<div class="page-wrap prose">
  <section id="question" aria-labelledby="question-title">
    <h2 id="question-title">The question</h2>
    <p>
      You need to put history into a sink that is already receiving live changes. How do you reload it without
      overwriting newer rows, bringing back deleted ones, or leaving rows behind that no longer exist at the source?
    </p>
    <p class="callout">
      A <a href="{{ '/glossary/' | url }}#backfill">backfill</a> is not a special kind of data. It is more
      at-least-once delivery, with one difference: it deliberately sends old content, and it sends it at the same time
      as the live stream. CDC delivery is at-least-once, and end-to-end exactly-once across independent systems is not
      achievable in general. What you can reach is <strong>exactly-once processing</strong>
      (<a href="{{ '/glossary/' | url }}#effectively-once">effectively-once</a>): at-least-once transport into an
      <a href="{{ '/glossary/' | url }}#idempotent-write">idempotent sink</a> keyed on the primary key and ordered by the
      <strong>source log position</strong> (<a href="{{ '/glossary/' | url }}#lsn-scn">LSN, SCN or binlog coordinate</a>),
      never by a timestamp, with deletes kept as <strong>delete markers</strong>. A backfill is safe exactly when the
      sink already has those three properties. Duplicates during the overlap are expected and harmless.
    </p>
    <p>
      This page is the task view. How an initial snapshot hands off to the stream, and how incremental snapshots use
      watermarks, are explained on <a href="{{ '/snapshotting/' | url }}">Snapshotting</a>
      (<a href="{{ '/snapshotting/' | url }}#watermarks">watermarks</a>,
      <a href="{{ '/snapshotting/' | url }}#idempotent">idempotent sink patterns</a>) and are not repeated here. The
      guard itself is derived on <a href="{{ '/partitioning/' | url }}#recon">Making Sinks Bulletproof</a>, and the
      delete marker on <a href="{{ '/deletes-stay-deleted/' | url }}">Deletes That Stay Deleted</a>.
    </p>
    <p class="muted">
      The Debezium, Kafka and Kafka Connect behaviour below was checked by reading the documentation on 2026-10-09
      (links at the end; the Debezium pages are the 3.7 documentation). Nothing was run against a live Debezium or
      Kafka. The SQL was run on SQLite in this site&rsquo;s unit tests. Where a behaviour is not stated in the pages
      read, the text says &ldquo;check your version&rdquo; instead of guessing.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="when" aria-labelledby="when-title">
    <h2 id="when-title">When and why you backfill</h2>
    <div style="overflow-x: auto" role="region" aria-label="Reasons to backfill" tabindex="0" data-table-scroll>
    <table>
      <caption>Five common reasons, and what each one changes about the job</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Reason</th>
          <th scope="col">What is missing in the sink</th>
          <th scope="col">What to watch</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">A new sink</th>
          <td>Everything that happened before the stream was connected.</td>
          <td>The easy case: the sink is empty, so only the overlap with the live stream matters.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">A new table or a changed capture set</th>
          <td>The existing rows of tables the connector did not capture before. Debezium names this case: the connector configuration is modified to capture a different set of tables.</td>
          <td>Snapshot only the new tables, not the whole database.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">A schema change</th>
          <td>Values for a new column in rows that have not changed since.</td>
          <td>Debezium&rsquo;s PostgreSQL connector does not support schema changes while an incremental snapshot is running. See <a href="{{ '/schema-evolution/' | url }}">Schema Evolution</a>.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">A bug fix in a transform or in the sink</th>
          <td>Rows that were written wrongly (data corruption is another reason Debezium lists for an ad hoc snapshot).</td>
          <td>A strict position guard skips events it has already seen, so replaying the <em>same</em> events through a fixed transform changes nothing. See <a href="#rule">the rule</a>.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">A lost or invalidated slot, or a purged log</th>
          <td>Every change between the last applied position and the new start, including deletes.</td>
          <td>The deletes. A snapshot cannot carry them. See <a href="#ghosts">rows the snapshot never mentions</a> and <a href="{{ '/postgres-replication-slots/' | url }}">Postgres Replication Slots &amp; WAL Growth</a>.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      A sixth reason is that the Kafka topics were deleted or lost retention. Debezium lists &ldquo;Kafka topics are
      deleted and must be rebuilt&rdquo; as a reason for an ad hoc snapshot. If the only thing wrong is the sink and the
      topic still holds the history, a replay from Kafka is cheaper than touching the source.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="choices" aria-labelledby="choices-title">
    <h2 id="choices-title">The five ways to reload</h2>
    <div style="overflow-x: auto" role="region" aria-label="Five ways to reload" tabindex="0" data-table-scroll>
    <table>
      <caption>What each choice re-emits and what it costs (Debezium 3.7 and Kafka 4.3 documentation)</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Choice</th>
          <th scope="col">Reads</th>
          <th scope="col">Streaming meanwhile</th>
          <th scope="col">Where the rows land</th>
          <th scope="col">Main limit</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">1. Re-snapshot the connector (<code>snapshot.mode</code>)</th>
          <td>The source tables, from the start.</td>
          <td>Streams from the snapshot position when it ends.</td>
          <td>The table topics.</td>
          <td>Coarse: the whole captured set. <code>initial</code> only runs when no offsets exist.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">2. Incremental snapshot (<code>execute-snapshot</code> signal)</th>
          <td>The chosen tables, in primary-key chunks, optionally filtered.</td>
          <td>Continues; chunk rows are reconciled with streamed changes in the connector.</td>
          <td>The existing table topics (the snapshot appends).</td>
          <td>Needs a signal channel; needs a usable key; chunks add read load.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">3. Blocking snapshot (<code>execute-snapshot</code>, type <code>blocking</code>)</th>
          <td>The chosen tables, like an initial snapshot.</td>
          <td>Paused until the snapshot completes.</td>
          <td>The existing table topics.</td>
          <td>Lag grows while streaming is paused; duplicates are possible around the start.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">4. Replay from Kafka</th>
          <td>The topic, from an earlier offset.</td>
          <td>Not applicable: the source is not touched.</td>
          <td>The sink only.</td>
          <td>Only as much history as retention or compaction left.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">5. Reload from the source by SQL</th>
          <td>The source tables, with your own query.</td>
          <td>Not applicable: you bypass Kafka.</td>
          <td>The sink only, so you stamp the position yourself.</td>
          <td>You own the boundary, the throttling and the verification.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      In every case the sink sees rows it may already have. That is the design, not a fault.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="modes" aria-labelledby="modes-title">
    <h2 id="modes-title">Choice 1: <code>snapshot.mode</code></h2>
    <p>
      The property decides whether a connector snapshots when it starts. The values below are the ones the Debezium 3.7
      PostgreSQL and MySQL pages list. Names have changed across releases: the MySQL page marks
      <code>schema_only</code> as deprecated in favour of <code>no_data</code> and <code>schema_only_recovery</code> as
      deprecated in favour of <code>recovery</code>. Check the page for your version and your connector before you set
      one.
    </p>
    <div style="overflow-x: auto" role="region" aria-label="snapshot.mode values" tabindex="0" data-table-scroll>
    <table>
      <caption>snapshot.mode values and their use for a reload</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Value</th>
          <th scope="col">What the documentation says</th>
          <th scope="col">Use for a reload</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row"><code>initial</code> (default)</th>
          <td>Snapshots when no offsets have been recorded for the logical server name, then streams.</td>
          <td>First deploy, or a connector that really has no offsets. It does not re-run on a connector that already has them.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row"><code>initial_only</code></th>
          <td>Snapshots, then stops without streaming.</td>
          <td>A one-off load for a batch target.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row"><code>always</code></th>
          <td>Snapshots every time the connector starts, then streams. The PostgreSQL page names two uses: WAL segments are known to be deleted, and a new primary was promoted.</td>
          <td>Recovery after a failover or a known gap. Every restart repeats the full load, so switch it back once the reload is done.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row"><code>when_needed</code></th>
          <td>Snapshots if it finds no offsets, or if a recorded offset points to a log position the server no longer has.</td>
          <td>Self-healing after a purged log. It will snapshot without asking; know that it will before you choose it.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row"><code>no_data</code> (MySQL: replaces <code>schema_only</code>)</th>
          <td>Never snapshots table data. On MySQL it still captures table structure. On PostgreSQL it streams from the stored LSN, or from where the slot was created, and says to use it only when all data of interest is still in the WAL.</td>
          <td>The sink was pre-loaded by other means, such as a SQL reload (choice 5). Not a backfill by itself.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row"><code>recovery</code> (MySQL; replaces <code>schema_only_recovery</code>)</th>
          <td>Rebuilds a lost or corrupted schema history topic from the current table structure. It does not use it for data; do not use it if schema changes were committed after the last shutdown.</td>
          <td>Repair, not reload. Listed here because the names are easy to confuse with a data snapshot.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row"><code>configuration_based</code>, <code>custom</code></th>
          <td>Control through properties with the prefix <code>snapshot.mode.configuration.based</code>, or through your own <code>Snapshotter</code> implementation.</td>
          <td>When none of the above fits. You now own the behaviour.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      What position do the snapshot rows carry? The MySQL page says all read events of an initial snapshot contain the
      same binlog position, the one read before the scan. The PostgreSQL page says streaming continues from the position
      read at the start of the snapshot, and its example event shows <code>source.lsn</code> and a
      <code>sequence</code> field (the last committed LSN, then the current LSN), but that example is a create event and
      the page does not say which <code>source.lsn</code> a read event carries: look at one in your own topic. Rows of an incremental snapshot have <code>op = 'r'</code> and <code>source.snapshot =
      "incremental"</code> in the Debezium example, which is how a sink can tell them apart for monitoring (not for
      ordering).
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="incremental" aria-labelledby="incremental-title">
    <h2 id="incremental-title">Choices 2 and 3: the <code>execute-snapshot</code> signal</h2>
    <p>
      Both are requested by writing a signal to the source database&rsquo;s signal table (the <code>source</code>
      channel) or to a Kafka signalling topic. The signal table is named by <code>signal.data.collection</code>, and
      <code>signal.enabled.channels</code> defaults to <code>source</code>. The table has the columns
      <code>id</code>, <code>type</code> and <code>data</code>. The statement below follows the Debezium PostgreSQL page;
      it is not run here.
    </p>
    <pre><code class="language-sql">-- From the Debezium documentation pattern. Not run in this site's tests.
INSERT INTO myschema.debezium_signal (id, type, data)
VALUES ('backfill-2026-10-09-1',
        'execute-snapshot',
        '{"data-collections": ["public.customers"],
          "type": "incremental",
          "additional-conditions": [
            {"data-collection": "public.customers", "filter": "id &gt;= 100000 AND id &lt; 200000"}
          ]}');</code></pre>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong><code>data-collections</code></strong> has no default. It is an array of table names or regular
        expressions, matched case-sensitively against <code>schema.table</code>. An empty array means no action.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong><code>type</code></strong> is <code>incremental</code> (the default when omitted) or
        <code>blocking</code>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong><code>additional-conditions</code></strong> appends a <code>WHERE</code> filter per table. This is how you
        backfill a key range or one tenant rather than the whole table. The sweep below must repeat the same filter. For a blocking snapshot, the filter is a full
        <code>SELECT</code>, the same form as <code>snapshot.select.<wbr>statement.<wbr>overrides</code>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Chunk size.</strong> <code>incremental.snapshot.<wbr>chunk.size</code> defaults to 1024 rows. A bigger chunk
        runs fewer queries and needs more memory to buffer.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Watermarks.</strong> <code>incremental.snapshot.<wbr>watermarking.strategy</code> is <code>insert_insert</code>
        (open and close entries per chunk in the signal table) or <code>insert_delete</code> (one entry, removed
        afterwards, which stops the signal table growing quickly). Setting <code>read.only</code> to true lets a PostgreSQL
        13 or later connector with a read-only connection run incremental snapshots without writing to the signal table.
        The mechanism itself is on <a href="{{ '/snapshotting/' | url }}#watermarks">Snapshotting</a>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Control.</strong> <code>pause-snapshot</code> and <code>resume-snapshot</code> signals pause and resume an
        incremental snapshot; <code>stop-snapshot</code> stops it, optionally for chosen tables. An interrupted
        incremental snapshot resumes where it stopped.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Where the rows go.</strong> When you run an ad hoc snapshot on an existing table, the connector appends
        the content to the topic that already exists. The backfill is therefore interleaved with live events in the
        same topic.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      <strong>Incremental versus blocking.</strong> An incremental snapshot keeps streaming; Debezium buffers each chunk
      and drops a buffered read when a streamed event for the same key arrives during the chunk&rsquo;s window, because the
      streamed event supersedes it. A blocking snapshot stops streaming, snapshots, then resumes, and the documentation
      warns that a delay between the signal and the actual start can make the connector emit some records that duplicate
      records the snapshot captured. Use blocking when you want the big table loaded faster than incremental chunks
      allow and can accept the pause.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="replay" aria-labelledby="replay-title">
    <h2 id="replay-title">Choice 4: replay from Kafka</h2>
    <p>
      When the topic still holds the history, you can rebuild the sink without touching the source. For a plain consumer
      group, Kafka&rsquo;s <code>kafka-consumer-groups.sh --reset-offsets</code> takes one group and a topic scope, offers
      <code>--to-earliest</code>, <code>--to-datetime</code>, <code>--to-offset</code>, <code>--shift-by</code> and
      <code>--from-file</code>, shows what it would do unless you add <code>--execute</code>, and requires the group&rsquo;s
      consumers to be inactive first. For a Kafka Connect sink connector, the REST API has offset management endpoints
      (KIP-875): <code>GET</code>, <code>DELETE</code> and <code>PATCH</code> on <code>/connectors/{name}/offsets</code>,
      which require the connector to exist and be in the <code>stopped</code> state. The same procedure is on
      <a href="{{ '/ops-offsets/' | url }}#rewind-title">Offsets &amp; Replays</a> and
      <a href="{{ '/reconciliation-surgery/' | url }}#kafka-connect-rest-title">Reconciliation and Offset Surgery</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Three limits decide whether the replay is enough:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Retention.</strong> With the default <code>delete</code> cleanup policy, old segments are discarded when
        the retention time or size limit is reached (<code>retention.ms</code> defaults to 7 days).
        <code>--to-earliest</code> means the earliest offset that still exists, which may not be the start of history.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Compaction.</strong> A compacted topic keeps at least the latest value for each key, so it can rebuild
        current state, and Kafka&rsquo;s design page describes exactly that use. It drops the intermediate versions.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Deletes on compacted topics.</strong> A record with a key and a null value is a delete marker that is
        itself cleaned out after <code>delete.retention.ms</code> (default 1 day). Kafka documents that setting as a bound
        on how long a consumer reading from offset 0 has to complete the read to get a valid final state. A sink that
        still holds old rows and replays a compacted topic can miss a delete whose marker has already been removed, and
        keep a row the source no longer has. Treat a compacted replay as a reload and run
        <a href="#ghosts">the sweep</a> afterwards.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="sql-reload" aria-labelledby="sql-reload-title">
    <h2 id="sql-reload-title">Choice 5: reload the sink from the source by SQL</h2>
    <p>
      Sometimes the sink and the source are reachable from one place (a replica, a foreign table, a batch job) and a
      query is simpler than a signal. The cost is that no connector chooses the boundary for you, and the whole
      correctness argument rests on it. The rule: <strong>stamp the loaded rows with a position at or below the first
      change of every transaction that is still open when you start the read</strong>.
    </p>
    <p>
      The reason is where a streamed position comes from. On PostgreSQL the <code>source.lsn</code> of a streamed event
      is the position of the change&rsquo;s WAL record, not of the commit (the Debezium 3.7 PostgreSQL page says it is
      &ldquo;the position in the write-ahead log where the transaction changes were written&rdquo;; in PostgreSQL&rsquo;s
      <code>logical.c</code> the change callback reports the change&rsquo;s own LSN and only the commit message uses the
      transaction&rsquo;s end LSN). A transaction that began before the stream&rsquo;s start position and commits after
      your read therefore arrives with positions <em>below</em> that start. If you stamped the loaded row with the start
      position, the loaded row would beat that change, which would then be skipped permanently.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Take a lower bound.</strong> For example the slot&rsquo;s <code>restart_lsn</code> read before the read
        starts (verify that on your version), or wait until no transaction older than the stream start is open before you
        read.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A new, empty target</strong> can take the lowest possible position, so loaded rows only fill keys the
        stream has not touched.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Per-key convergence only.</strong> The load gives no transactional consistency across rows while it runs:
        two rows changed by one source transaction can be visible at different versions until the stream catches up.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      This is my reasoning from those two sources, not a vendor procedure. The unit tests reproduce the straddling
      transaction with the unsafe stamp and with a lower bound; prove it in staging with a long transaction that starts
      before the read and commits after it (the runbook has the step). Debezium&rsquo;s own handoff is described as
      streaming from the position read at the start of the snapshot; that page describes no sink-side comparison.
    </p>
    <p>
      Do not stamp the loaded rows with the position <em>after</em> the read, or with a timestamp. A position taken after
      the read can be above a change the read did not see, and then the older loaded row beats the newer streamed one.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="rule" aria-labelledby="rule-title">
    <h2 id="rule-title">The correctness rule</h2>
    <p>
      A backfill produces events that <strong>must not overwrite newer streamed changes</strong>. The sink enforces that
      itself; it cannot rely on the order in which the reload and the stream happen to arrive. The statement is the same
      one used for the stream, applied to backfill rows too:
    </p>
    <pre><code class="language-sql">CREATE TABLE customers (
  id         BIGINT  PRIMARY KEY,
  email      TEXT,                          -- NULL on a delete
  is_deleted BOOLEAN NOT NULL DEFAULT FALSE,
  source_lsn BIGINT  NOT NULL               -- position of the change that wrote this row
);

-- Apply ONE row, from the stream or from a backfill. For a backfill row,
-- source_lsn is the snapshot's boundary position (see the sections above).
INSERT INTO customers (id, email, is_deleted, source_lsn)
VALUES (7, 'a@example.com', FALSE, 150)
ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE
SET email      = EXCLUDED.email,
    is_deleted = EXCLUDED.is_deleted,
    source_lsn = EXCLUDED.source_lsn
WHERE customers.source_lsn &lt; EXCLUDED.source_lsn;</code></pre>
    <p>
      The strict <code>&lt;</code> does three jobs. A backfill row with a lower position than the stored one is skipped, so
      it cannot overwrite a newer streamed change. A delete marker that is stored with a position blocks a backfill row
      from the time before the delete, so the key cannot come back. And applying the same row twice does nothing, because
      the second copy has an equal, not a higher, position. The unit tests for this page apply the same seven rows in
      every one of the 5,040 possible orders, and then replay them again in reverse; every run ends in the same table.
    </p>
    <p>
      Three consequences are easy to miss:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>The backfill rows must carry positions, in the same unit the stream uses.</strong> A reload that stamps
        the load time, or that writes with a plain upsert, overwrites newer rows. A plain upsert keyed on the primary key,
        such as the Debezium JDBC sink&rsquo;s <code>insert.mode=upsert</code>, is idempotent for replays of the <em>same</em> event;
        the pages I read describe it as insert-or-update on the key and mention no comparison of positions, so an older
        event arriving later still replaces a newer row. Compare
        <a href="{{ '/which-row-wins/' | url }}">Which Row Wins in Your Target</a> for the setting in each warehouse.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The guard skips corrections that keep the same position.</strong> If you reload to fix a transform bug,
        the corrected rows carry the positions the bad ones had, so the strict guard skips them. For a content fix, load
        into a new table with the corrected transform, let it catch up from the stream, check it, and switch readers over.
        Do not loosen the guard on the live table to <code>&lt;=</code>: a row with an equal position would then replace the stored one, so a late backfill row stamped at the shared boundary would beat a sweep marker or a streamed change at that position.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Ties need an ordinal.</strong> If two changes to one key can share a position, compare the pair
        (position, ordinal). See <a href="{{ '/partitioning/' | url }}#recon">Making Sinks Bulletproof</a>.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="ghosts" aria-labelledby="ghosts-title">
    <h2 id="ghosts-title">Rows the snapshot never mentions</h2>
    <p>
      A snapshot emits the rows that exist when it reads. A row deleted at the source while the sink was missing events
      is not in the snapshot, and its delete event died with the log, so the old row stays live in the sink. Nothing
      about the guard can fix that: it only compares rows it receives.
    </p>
    <p>
      The remedy is a sweep: for the keys the backfill covered, mark every live sink row that is not in the source as
      deleted, with a position at the boundary. The marker keeps the key deleted if an old update turns up later.
    </p>
    <pre><code class="language-sql">-- backfill_keys: the keys the backfill read from the source, loaded into the sink's database.
-- :boundary is the position the backfill rows carry; :lo and :hi bound the keys the backfill covered.
UPDATE customers
SET email = NULL, is_deleted = TRUE, source_lsn = :boundary
WHERE NOT is_deleted
  AND source_lsn &lt; :boundary
  AND id BETWEEN :lo AND :hi
  -- Repeat the backfill's additional-conditions filter here, if it had one:
  -- AND tenant_id = 5
  AND id NOT IN (SELECT id FROM backfill_keys);</code></pre>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Scope it exactly like the backfill.</strong> Without the <code>id BETWEEN</code> bound, a filtered
        backfill would mark every row it did not read as deleted. A key range is not enough when the backfill also had an
        <code>additional-conditions</code> filter: a tenant-5 backfill swept with only <code>lo = 10</code> and
        <code>hi = 900</code> would mark live tenant-6 rows in that range as deleted. Repeat the filter in the sweep (the
        commented line), which means the sink needs that column.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The key list must be complete for the scope.</strong> Under the runbook the backfill is applied first, so
        backfilled rows already sit at the boundary and the sweep leaves them alone. The hazard is a key that is live at the
        source but missing from both the list and the load (the list was built with a different filter or in a different
        pass): the sweep marks it deleted at the boundary, and a later row for it at that same position is skipped by the
        strict guard, so it stays deleted until the stream next changes it. The unit tests demonstrate it, and also
        that a sweep which runs before the load, with a list that lacks a key, has the same effect. Read the list from the
        source in one pass.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Use the same lower bound for <code>:boundary</code>.</strong> With the stamp rule from
        <a href="#sql-reload">choice 5</a>, a higher boundary would sweep a row that a transaction straddling the read
        inserted and the stream already applied.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Why a key list and not the position alone.</strong> Rows from an incremental snapshot may not all carry
        one boundary (check a sample), and rows the connector drops because the stream changed them during a chunk window
        never reach the sink as snapshot rows. The position test still matters: it protects rows the stream wrote after
        the list was read.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Check the count first.</strong> Run the same <code>WHERE</code> as a <code>SELECT COUNT(*)</code> and read
        the number before you update. A large number after a short outage means the scope or the boundary is wrong.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The delete marker itself, and when a marker may finally be purged, are covered on
      <a href="{{ '/deletes-stay-deleted/' | url }}">Deletes That Stay Deleted</a>. A purge bound must account for a
      backfill: the lowest position a replay source can still deliver now includes the snapshot you just ran.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="idempotent" aria-labelledby="idempotent-title">
    <h2 id="idempotent-title">Re-running a backfill, and the overlap</h2>
    <p>
      A backfill that fails halfway has to be restartable, and an operator will run it twice by mistake. With the guard
      from <a href="#rule">the rule</a>, a second run of the same rows does nothing, and the sweep is idempotent as well:
      it only touches rows that are still live and below the boundary. The unit tests run both twice and compare the
      tables.
    </p>
    <p>
      Expect duplicates in these places and design for them rather than hunting for them:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>An incremental snapshot chunk that is retried after a failure.</li>
      <li>The window around a blocking snapshot&rsquo;s start, which the documentation says can duplicate rows.</li>
      <li>A change that is in both the snapshot rows and the stream. The guard keeps one version.</li>
      <li>A replay that starts earlier than strictly necessary, on purpose, to be safe.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      None of these needs a deduplication step in front of the sink, because the sink already compares positions.
      <a href="{{ '/glossary/' | url }}#deduplication">Deduplication</a> by event id, which an outbox relay uses, is a
      different tool for a different problem.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="cost" aria-labelledby="cost-title">
    <h2 id="cost-title">Cost and throttling</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Read load on the source.</strong> Chunk queries compete with application traffic. Smaller chunks mean
        shorter queries and more of them; <code>incremental.snapshot.<wbr>chunk.size</code> (1024) is the lever. For the initial
        snapshot, <code>snapshot.fetch.size</code> (10240 rows per batch by default) and <code>snapshot.max.threads</code>
        (1 by default; above 1 the connector splits tables by primary-key range) set the pressure. A table without a
        primary key is processed as a single chunk on one thread.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Pausing.</strong> You can pause and resume an incremental snapshot with signals instead of killing it,
        for example at the start of a peak.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Streaming lag.</strong> A blocking snapshot stops streaming for its duration, so lag on every captured
        table grows until it ends. On PostgreSQL my reading is that a connector that is not consuming the slot also
        lets WAL accumulate, so watch the retained WAL as described on
        <a href="{{ '/postgres-replication-slots/' | url }}">Postgres Replication Slots &amp; WAL Growth</a>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Topic size.</strong> An ad hoc snapshot appends the table to its existing topic. Check that retention
        and disk can hold the live events and a full copy of the table.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Sink write rate.</strong> A full reload is a burst of upserts. Size batches and warehouse capacity for it,
        and run it off peak. The guard makes the work harmless, not free: a skipped row still costs a lookup.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      This page gives no cost figures. Any number would depend on your source, sink and prices.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="verify" aria-labelledby="verify-title">
    <h2 id="verify-title">Verify the result</h2>
    <p>
      Do not stop at &ldquo;the job finished&rdquo;. The checks below fail on the three ways a backfill goes wrong:
      missing rows, stale rows and ghost rows. More patterns are on
      <a href="{{ '/test-your-pipeline/' | url }}#reconciliation-title">Testing a CDC Pipeline</a>,
      <a href="{{ '/reconciliation-surgery/' | url }}#checksum-verification-title">Reconciliation and Offset Surgery</a>
      and the <a href="{{ '/merge-cookbook/' | url }}">Merge cookbook</a>.
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong>Counts by key range.</strong> Count live sink rows per bucket and compare with the same count at the
        source. Exclude marker rows (<code>is_deleted</code>) on the sink side.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Checksums by key range.</strong> Hash a payload per bucket on both sides. The hash function is
        database-specific; the example below is PostgreSQL syntax.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Suspect rows.</strong> After a complete backfill and sweep, no live row in scope should sit below the
        boundary. A non-zero count means ghosts, an incomplete load or the wrong boundary.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Max position per key.</strong> The highest stored position per bucket should be at or above the boundary
        and no higher than the newest position the stream has delivered.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Spot-check recent keys.</strong> Change a few rows at the source during the backfill on purpose, and
        confirm the sink holds the newest value, not the snapshot&rsquo;s.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <pre><code class="language-sql">-- Counts and the highest position per 1000-key bucket (runs on PostgreSQL and SQLite).
SELECT id / 1000 AS bucket, COUNT(*) AS live_rows, MAX(source_lsn) AS max_lsn
FROM customers
WHERE NOT is_deleted
GROUP BY id / 1000
ORDER BY bucket;

-- Suspect rows after the sweep: should be 0 (same predicate as the sweep).
SELECT COUNT(*) AS suspects
FROM customers
WHERE NOT is_deleted
  AND source_lsn &lt; :boundary
  AND id BETWEEN :lo AND :hi;

-- Payload checksum per bucket, PostgreSQL syntax. Run the equivalent at the source and compare.
SELECT id / 1000 AS bucket, COUNT(*) AS live_rows,
       md5(string_agg(id::text || ':' || COALESCE(email, ''), ',' ORDER BY id)) AS checksum
FROM customers
WHERE NOT is_deleted
GROUP BY id / 1000
ORDER BY bucket;</code></pre>
    <p class="muted small">
      The first two queries are executed by the unit tests on SQLite. The checksum query uses <code>md5</code> and
      <code>string_agg</code>, which SQLite does not have; I ran it once by hand on a PostgreSQL-compatible engine
      (PGlite) outside the test suite, so treat it as lightly checked. Checksums also have blind spots: columns you do
      not hash, and rows the source changed since you read. See the lies section of
      <a href="{{ '/test-your-pipeline/' | url }}#reconciliation-title">Testing a CDC Pipeline</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="runbook" aria-labelledby="runbook-title">
    <h2 id="runbook-title">Runbook: reload history without breaking the sink</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Name the reason</strong> from <a href="#when">the table</a>, and the scope: which tables, which key range.</li>
      <li><strong>Check the sink first.</strong> It must key on the primary key, carry a never-<code>NULL</code> position column, compare positions with a strict <code>&lt;</code>, and keep deletes as markers. Fix that before any load. If it does not, do not start.</li>
      <li><strong>Rehearse in staging</strong> with a copy of the table. Include a long transaction that starts before the snapshot and commits after it, and confirm that the changed row ends with the newer value.</li>
      <li><strong>Check the log is still there</strong> from the position you need. On MySQL, binlog retention: a snapshot that runs longer than it loses the handoff. On PostgreSQL the slot keeps WAL for you, so the risk is the opposite: disk growth while the snapshot runs, or the slot being invalidated by <code>max_slot_wal_keep_size</code> (see <a href="{{ '/postgres-replication-slots/' | url }}">the slots runbook</a>). In Kafka, topic retention.</li>
      <li><strong>Pick the choice</strong> from <a href="#choices">the table</a> and write the signal or the command down before you run it, including the filter.</li>
      <li><strong>Inspect a sample</strong> of the first backfill rows: <code>op</code>, <code>source.snapshot</code> and the position they carry. If the position is not what the guard expects, stop.</li>
      <li><strong>Set the pace</strong> (chunk size, threads, fetch size), and agree who may pause the snapshot if the source is under load.</li>
      <li><strong>Run it</strong> and watch lag, retained WAL, topic size and the sink&rsquo;s write latency.</li>
      <li><strong>Wait until the sink has applied every backfill row.</strong> Consumer lag on the topic is zero and the snapshot is reported complete.</li>
      <li><strong>Sweep</strong> rows the source no longer has, using a complete key list for the same scope, and read the count before you commit it.</li>
      <li><strong>Verify</strong> with the counts, checksums, suspect-row query and spot checks above. Record the boundary, the counts and the checks.</li>
      <li><strong>Put the settings back.</strong> A <code>snapshot.mode</code> of <code>always</code> repeats the full load on every restart. Remove temporary signal permissions and filters.</li>
      <li><strong>Recompute the purge bound</strong> for delete markers, since the backfill just added a replay source.</li>
      <li><strong>Check the positions are comparable.</strong> My reasoning, not a vendor statement: positions only compare within one source log lineage. After a source rebuild, restore or migration, a new cluster can restart its LSN or binlog numbering below the <code>source_lsn</code> already stored, and the guard would then skip every new change. Start a fresh target, or re-key the positions. A MySQL position is a (file, position) pair, so encode it into one number that sorts the same way.</li>
    </ol>
  </section>

